WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.627 --> 00:00:01.970
- [Narrator] “Goosebumps”
is one of the best selling

00:00:01.970 --> 00:00:04.365
book series of all time.

00:00:04.365 --> 00:00:06.434
And this man is responsible for each

00:00:06.434 --> 00:00:11.310
and every terrifying,
spine-tingling, horror-filled story.

00:00:11.310 --> 00:00:12.735
- Hi, I'm R. L. Stine.

00:00:12.735 --> 00:00:15.258
I write the “Goosebumps” books.

00:00:15.258 --> 00:00:16.876
- [Narrator] Huh, maybe he's not so scary?

00:00:16.876 --> 00:00:19.949
- [Author] I actually
started as a humor writer.

00:00:19.949 --> 00:00:22.911
- [Narrator] Well, what do you know?

00:00:22.911 --> 00:00:27.133
In 1992, Scholastic released
the very first “Goosebumps” book,

00:00:27.133 --> 00:00:29.893
but way before that,
the young Bob Stine was

00:00:29.893 --> 00:00:31.192
into writing jokes.

00:00:31.192 --> 00:00:34.753
- I started writing funny stuff
when I was nine years old.

00:00:34.753 --> 00:00:37.551
All I ever wanted to be was funny.

00:00:37.551 --> 00:00:40.755
When I came to New York, I
had my own humor magazine

00:00:40.755 --> 00:00:43.310
and after 10 years I
thought, "Gee, I've done

00:00:43.310 --> 00:00:44.811
"what I always wanted to do.

00:00:44.811 --> 00:00:47.247
"I guess I'll coast the rest of my life."

00:00:47.247 --> 00:00:50.287
I had no idea what was in store for me.

00:00:50.287 --> 00:00:53.531
- [Narrator] And then,
things started to get spooky.

00:00:53.531 --> 00:00:57.348
- I had lunch with the editorial
director at Scholastic.

00:00:57.348 --> 00:01:00.837
She'd had a fight with a YA horror writer

00:01:00.837 --> 00:01:04.528
and she said, "You could
write a good YA horror novel."

00:01:04.528 --> 00:01:06.795
I said, "Okay, no problem, you got it."

00:01:06.795 --> 00:01:09.633
And I went home and wrote this novel

00:01:09.633 --> 00:01:11.948
and it was a number one best seller.

00:01:11.948 --> 00:01:16.134
I thought, "Oh, wait a minute,
forget the funny stuff."

00:01:16.134 --> 00:01:18.409
And I've been scary ever since.

00:01:18.409 --> 00:01:20.579
- [Narrator] That book marked
the start of R. L. Stine's

00:01:20.579 --> 00:01:22.178
horror writing career.

00:01:22.178 --> 00:01:25.546
And he eventually wrote an
entire series called “Goosebumps.”

00:01:25.546 --> 00:01:28.059
But it wasn't an immediate success.

00:01:28.059 --> 00:01:31.144
- They went out and they
just sat on the shelves.

00:01:31.144 --> 00:01:32.238
They didn't sell.

00:01:32.238 --> 00:01:35.041
Two months goes by, three months, nothing.

00:01:35.041 --> 00:01:37.411
We figured that's it, didn't work out.

00:01:37.411 --> 00:01:38.896
And then something happened.

00:01:38.896 --> 00:01:40.072
It's a mystery.

00:01:40.072 --> 00:01:42.667
Kids discovered it somehow.

00:01:42.667 --> 00:01:44.209
Nobody knew me really.

00:01:44.209 --> 00:01:47.663
There was no hype, no
advertising, no publicity.

00:01:47.663 --> 00:01:48.800
There was nothing.

00:01:48.800 --> 00:01:50.867
Just kids telling kids.

00:01:50.867 --> 00:01:53.056
It was just, you know,
one of these amazing,

00:01:53.056 --> 00:01:55.250
lucky things that happened.

00:01:55.250 --> 00:01:57.689
- [Narrator] Now after 25
years, “Goosebumps” is scaring

00:01:57.689 --> 00:01:59.715
a whole new generation of kids.

00:01:59.715 --> 00:02:01.990
- It's a really nice
thing to be able to write

00:02:01.990 --> 00:02:03.813
for different generations.

00:02:03.813 --> 00:02:06.862
At first I was shocked when
adults starting showing up

00:02:06.862 --> 00:02:08.283
at my book signing.

00:02:08.283 --> 00:02:10.350
I'd say, "What are you doing here?"

00:02:10.350 --> 00:02:12.500
They'd say we loved you when we were kids.

00:02:12.500 --> 00:02:16.359
And I realized I'm nostalgia to them.

00:02:16.359 --> 00:02:19.281
I think you kind of
remember that tingle of fear

00:02:19.281 --> 00:02:22.048
that you had when you were a kid.

00:02:22.048 --> 00:02:23.754
- [Narrator] But with the
success of “Goosebumps,”

00:02:23.754 --> 00:02:26.434
does R. L. Stine ever miss
the chance to be funny?

00:02:26.434 --> 00:02:28.459
- There's something weird with my brain

00:02:28.459 --> 00:02:31.587
because horror always makes me laugh.

00:02:31.587 --> 00:02:34.351
It's the same visceral reaction.

00:02:34.351 --> 00:02:36.261
That's what I try to do in my books.

00:02:36.261 --> 00:02:38.043
People think if you're a horror writer,

00:02:38.043 --> 00:02:42.100
you're scary all the time and
your whole life is horror,

00:02:42.100 --> 00:02:44.493
but I did my own late night comedy show

00:02:44.493 --> 00:02:47.293
at Upright Citizen's Brigade last year.

00:02:47.293 --> 00:02:48.947
- [Interviewer] Are you
doing one this year, too?

00:02:48.947 --> 00:02:51.829
- I'm waiting by the
phone, no one's called me.

00:02:51.829 --> 00:02:53.996
I don't know, I'm waiting.

00:02:56.455 --> 00:03:00.122
That was it, that might
be my comedy career.

